\subsection{Download Completion Detection}

When all pieces have been downloaded and verified, the client must notify the
tracker with an [[event=completed]] announce. This signals our transition from
\emph{leecher} (downloading) to \emph{seeder} (having all pieces). Although this
client does not implement seeding per the project's non-goals, correctly sending
the completed event is important for tracker statistics and swarm health metrics.

The completion detection logic is triggered after each piece is verified. When
the last piece is marked as verified, the piece manager's [[is\_complete()]]
method returns true, and we:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Log the completion with total download time and average speed.
\item Send a tracker announce with [[event=completed]].
\item Set an internal flag to prevent further piece requests.
\end{enumerate}

The completion announce follows the same best-effort pattern as the stopped
announce: we attempt to notify the tracker but don't fail the download if the
announce fails. Tracker notification is important but not critical---the tracker
will eventually update our status based on subsequent regular announces.

\subsubsection{The Completed Announce}

The [[send\_completed\_announce]] function is structurally similar to
[[send\_stopped\_announce]] but uses [[TrackerEvent::Completed]].

<<completion announce>>=
/// Timeout for completion announce, in seconds.
///
/// We use the same short timeout as shutdown announces because the user
/// has finished downloading and shouldn't have to wait long for tracker
/// communication.
pub const COMPLETION_ANNOUNCE_TIMEOUT_SECS: u64 = 10;

/// Result of a completion detection check.
///
/// This enum captures what happened when we checked for download completion
/// after a piece was verified. It helps the caller understand whether the
/// download just completed and what action was taken.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
pub enum CompletionResult {
    /// The download was already complete before this check.
    AlreadyComplete,

    /// The download is still in progress; not all pieces are verified.
    StillDownloading {
        /// Number of verified pieces.
        verified: usize,
        /// Total number of pieces.
        total: u32,
    },

    /// The download just completed with this piece verification.
    JustCompleted {
        /// Total download time.
        elapsed: Duration,
        /// Average download speed in bytes per second.
        bytes_per_second: f64,
    },
}

/// Send a "completed" event to the tracker when download finishes.
///
/// This function notifies the tracker that we have finished downloading all
/// pieces. The tracker uses this to update statistics and potentially provide
/// us to other peers as a seeder (though we don't implement seeding).
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `announce_url` - The tracker announce URL
/// * `info_hash` - The 20-byte info hash
/// * `peer_id` - Our 20-byte peer identifier
/// * `port` - The port we are listening on
/// * `uploaded` - Total bytes uploaded during this session
/// * `downloaded` - Total bytes downloaded during this session
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(())` if the tracker acknowledged our completed event
/// * `Err(TrackerError)` if the announce failed (logged as warning)
///
/// # Logging
///
/// - INFO: "Sending completed announce to tracker"
/// - INFO: "Tracker notified of completion" (on success)
/// - WARN: "Failed to send completed announce: {error}" (on failure)
pub async fn send_completed_announce(
    announce_url: &str,
    info_hash: &[u8; 20],
    peer_id: &[u8; 20],
    port: u16,
    uploaded: u64,
    downloaded: u64,
) -> Result<(), TrackerError> {
    info!("Sending completed announce to tracker");

    // Build the request with event=completed
    // left=0 because we have all pieces
    let request = TrackerRequest {
        info_hash: *info_hash,
        peer_id: *peer_id,
        port,
        uploaded,
        downloaded,
        left: 0, // All pieces verified
        event: Some(TrackerEvent::Completed),
        compact: true,
    };

    // Build the URL
    let url = build_announce_url(announce_url, &request);

    // Send with timeout
    let client = reqwest::Client::builder()
        .timeout(Duration::from_secs(COMPLETION_ANNOUNCE_TIMEOUT_SECS))
        .build()
        .map_err(TrackerError::TrackerHttpError)?;

    let response = match tokio::time::timeout(
        Duration::from_secs(COMPLETION_ANNOUNCE_TIMEOUT_SECS),
        client.get(&url).send(),
    ).await {
        Ok(Ok(resp)) => resp,
        Ok(Err(e)) => {
            warn!("Failed to send completed announce: {}", e);
            return Err(TrackerError::TrackerHttpError(e));
        }
        Err(_) => {
            warn!("Completed announce timed out after {} seconds", COMPLETION_ANNOUNCE_TIMEOUT_SECS);
            return Err(TrackerError::TrackerTimeout);
        }
    };

    // Check status
    if response.status().is_success() {
        info!("Tracker notified of completion");
        Ok(())
    } else {
        let status = response.status();
        warn!("Tracker returned error status {} for completed announce", status);
        Err(TrackerError::TrackerInvalidResponse(
            format!("tracker returned HTTP {}", status)
        ))
    }
}

impl Client {
    /// Check for download completion after a piece is verified.
    ///
    /// This method should be called after each successful piece verification.
    /// It checks if all pieces are now verified, and if so:
    ///
    /// 1. Logs the completion with timing and speed statistics
    /// 2. Sets the internal `download_complete` flag
    /// 3. Returns `JustCompleted` with the statistics
    ///
    /// The caller is responsible for sending the completed announce to the
    /// tracker based on the return value. This separation allows the caller
    /// to handle the async tracker communication appropriately.
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `AlreadyComplete` - Download was already marked complete
    /// * `StillDownloading` - Not all pieces are verified yet
    /// * `JustCompleted` - Download just completed with this check
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```ignore
    /// // After successfully verifying a piece:
    /// match client.check_completion() {
    ///     CompletionResult::JustCompleted { elapsed, bytes_per_second } => {
    ///         // Send completed announce to tracker
    ///         send_completed_announce(...).await;
    ///     }
    ///     CompletionResult::StillDownloading { verified, total } => {
    ///         // Continue downloading
    ///     }
    ///     CompletionResult::AlreadyComplete => {
    ///         // Ignore, already handled
    ///     }
    /// }
    /// ```
    pub fn check_completion(&mut self) -> CompletionResult {
        // Already marked complete
        if self.download_complete {
            return CompletionResult::AlreadyComplete;
        }

        // Check if all pieces are verified
        if !self.piece_manager.is_complete() {
            let verified = self.piece_manager.verified_count();
            let total = self.piece_manager.num_pieces();
            return CompletionResult::StillDownloading { verified, total };
        }

        // Download just completed!
        self.download_complete = true;

        let elapsed = self.start_time.elapsed();
        let total_bytes = self.metainfo.total_length as f64;
        let bytes_per_second = if elapsed.as_secs_f64() > 0.0 {
            total_bytes / elapsed.as_secs_f64()
        } else {
            total_bytes // Instant completion
        };

        // Format speed for logging
        let speed_str = if bytes_per_second >= 1_000_000.0 {
            format!("{:.2} MB/s", bytes_per_second / 1_000_000.0)
        } else if bytes_per_second >= 1_000.0 {
            format!("{:.2} KB/s", bytes_per_second / 1_000.0)
        } else {
            format!("{:.0} B/s", bytes_per_second)
        };

        info!(
            "Download complete! {} in {:.1}s ({}) - {} pieces verified",
            format_bytes(self.metainfo.total_length),
            elapsed.as_secs_f64(),
            speed_str,
            self.piece_manager.num_pieces()
        );

        CompletionResult::JustCompleted { elapsed, bytes_per_second }
    }

    /// Mark the completed announce as sent.
    ///
    /// This should be called after successfully sending the completed announce
    /// to the tracker, or after deciding not to retry a failed announce.
    pub fn mark_completed_announced(&mut self) {
        self.completed_announced = true;
    }
}

/// Format a byte count as a human-readable string.
fn format_bytes(bytes: u64) -> String {
    if bytes >= 1_073_741_824 {
        format!("{:.2} GB", bytes as f64 / 1_073_741_824.0)
    } else if bytes >= 1_048_576 {
        format!("{:.2} MB", bytes as f64 / 1_048_576.0)
    } else if bytes >= 1024 {
        format!("{:.2} KB", bytes as f64 / 1024.0)
    } else {
        format!("{} bytes", bytes)
    }
}
@

The [[check\_completion]] method is designed to be called synchronously after each
piece verification. It returns a [[CompletionResult]] enum that tells the caller
what happened, allowing the caller to handle the async tracker communication
appropriately.

Note that we separate the completion check from the tracker announce. This keeps
the [[check\_completion]] method synchronous and fast, while giving the caller
control over when and how to send the async tracker request. This is important
for integration with the main event loop.

