\subsection{Peer Connection Manager}

The [[PeerConnectionManager]] orchestrates the pool of active peer connections.
In a BitTorrent swarm, we typically want to maintain connections to many peers
simultaneously---the PRD specifies 30 concurrent connections by default. This
presents several challenges:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Connection lifecycle}: Peers come and go. We need to track which
  peers we're connected to and replace disconnected ones with fresh addresses
  from our available pool.

\item \textbf{Address management}: The tracker returns a list of peer addresses,
  but we can't connect to all of them at once. We queue addresses and draw from
  the pool as slots become available.

\item \textbf{Duplicate prevention}: The same peer address might appear in
  multiple tracker responses. We must not create duplicate connections.

\item \textbf{State isolation}: Each peer has its own 4-bit choke/interest
  state machine and bitfield. The connection manager owns this per-peer state.
\end{enumerate}

The [[PeerConnectionManager]] addresses these concerns by maintaining two
collections: a map of active connections keyed by socket address, and a queue
of available addresses not yet connected.

\subsubsection{Design Decisions}

We use [[VecDeque]] for available peers rather than [[HashSet]] for several
reasons:

\begin{itemize}
\item FIFO ordering: Connect to peers in the order received from tracker
\item Efficient front/back operations: [[push\_back]] and [[pop\_front]] are O(1)
\item Simpler implementation: No need for random selection or ordering logic
\end{itemize}

The connection map uses [[SocketAddr]] as the key because a peer is uniquely
identified by their IP:port combination. Using [[SocketAddr]] also makes it
trivial to detect duplicate connection attempts.

\subsubsection{Constants and Connection Utilities}

Connection timeouts are essential for robustness in peer-to-peer networks.
Without explicit timeouts, the operating system's default TCP connection
timeout (often 2+ minutes on Linux) would cause the client to block indefinitely
when attempting to connect to offline or firewalled peers.

The [[connect\_with\_timeout]] function wraps [[TcpStream::connect]] with a
10-second timeout as specified in the PRD. This timeout balances two concerns:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Responsiveness}: We don't want to wait too long for unresponsive
  peers when there are many other peers available in the swarm.
\item \textbf{Reliability}: We don't want to give up too quickly on peers that
  may simply be on high-latency networks. Ten seconds is sufficient for even
  intercontinental connections.
\end{enumerate}

The function returns [[PeerError::Timeout]] when the timeout elapses and
[[PeerError::IoError]] for immediate failures like connection refused.

<<peer connection manager>>=
use crate::messages::PeerError;
use tokio::net::TcpStream;
use tokio::time::timeout;

/// Default maximum number of concurrent peer connections.
///
/// The BitTorrent protocol benefits from connecting to many peers
/// simultaneously to maximize download parallelism. However, too many
/// connections can exhaust system resources (file descriptors, memory)
/// and overwhelm the network stack.
///
/// 30 is a common default that balances parallelism with resource usage.
/// This can be configured per-manager if needed.
pub const DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS: usize = 30;

/// Timeout in seconds for establishing TCP connections.
///
/// If a peer doesn't respond within this time, the connection attempt
/// fails with `PeerError::Timeout`. The PRD specifies 10 seconds.
pub const CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECS: u64 = 10;

/// Establishes a TCP connection to a peer with timeout.
///
/// Uses a 10-second timeout to prevent hanging on unresponsive peers.
/// The timeout is configurable via the `CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECS` constant.
///
/// # Why Timeouts Matter
///
/// BitTorrent peers are unreliable by nature. A peer address from the tracker
/// might be:
/// - Offline (machine powered down)
/// - Behind a firewall that drops packets silently
/// - On a slow or congested network
/// - Running but not accepting connections
///
/// Without a timeout, `TcpStream::connect` could block indefinitely waiting
/// for a response that never comes. The OS typically has a very long default
/// timeout (often 2+ minutes). A 10-second timeout is long enough for legitimate
/// connections over slow networks but short enough to move on to other peers
/// when one is unresponsive.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `addr` - The socket address of the peer to connect to
///
/// # Returns
///
/// * `Ok(TcpStream)` - A connected TCP stream ready for the handshake
/// * `Err(PeerError::Timeout)` - Connection not established within 10 seconds
/// * `Err(PeerError::IoError)` - Other connection failures (refused, unreachable)
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```no_run
/// use std::net::SocketAddr;
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::connect_with_timeout;
///
/// async fn example() {
///     let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
///     match connect_with_timeout(addr).await {
///         Ok(stream) => println!("Connected to {}", addr),
///         Err(e) => println!("Failed to connect: {}", e),
///     }
/// }
/// ```
pub async fn connect_with_timeout(addr: SocketAddr) -> Result<TcpStream, PeerError> {
    let connect_timeout = Duration::from_secs(CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECS);

    match timeout(connect_timeout, TcpStream::connect(addr)).await {
        Ok(Ok(stream)) => Ok(stream),
        Ok(Err(io_err)) => Err(PeerError::IoError(io_err)),
        Err(_elapsed) => Err(PeerError::Timeout),
    }
}

/// A simple wrapper around a peer connection.
///
/// Currently holds the socket address and per-peer protocol state.
/// In future tasks (task-0112), this will be expanded to include the
/// actual TCP stream for communication.
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct PeerConnection {
    /// The socket address (IP:port) of this peer.
    pub addr: SocketAddr,

    /// Per-peer protocol state (choke/interest state machine, bitfield).
    pub state: PeerState,
    // Note: stream field will be added in task-0112 when we implement
    // actual TCP connections
}

/// Manages the pool of active peer connections.
///
/// The `PeerConnectionManager` is responsible for:
/// - Tracking active connections and their state
/// - Maintaining a queue of available peer addresses
/// - Connecting to new peers when slots are available
/// - Providing access to peer state for message handling
///
/// # Connection Strategy
///
/// The manager implements a simple connection strategy:
/// 1. Maintain up to `max_connections` active connections
/// 2. When a slot is available and peers are queued, connect to the next one
/// 3. When a connection drops, it's removed and the slot becomes available
///
/// # Thread Safety
///
/// This struct is designed for single-threaded use within the main event loop.
/// For multi-threaded access, wrap in appropriate synchronization primitives.
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```
/// use std::net::SocketAddr;
/// use literate_bittorrent::client::{PeerConnectionManager, DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS};
///
/// let manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
///     [0xAA; 20],  // our peer_id
///     [0xBB; 20],  // info_hash
///     100,         // num_pieces
///     DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS,
/// );
///
/// // Initially empty
/// assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 0);
/// assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 0);
/// ```
#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct PeerConnectionManager {
    /// Active peer connections, keyed by socket address.
    ///
    /// Using `HashMap` provides O(1) lookup and prevents duplicate
    /// connections to the same address.
    connections: HashMap<SocketAddr, PeerConnection>,

    /// Queue of peer addresses available for connection.
    ///
    /// These addresses came from tracker responses but we haven't
    /// connected to them yet. FIFO ordering ensures fairness.
    available_peers: VecDeque<SocketAddr>,

    /// Our 20-byte peer ID for handshakes.
    peer_id: [u8; 20],

    /// The 20-byte info hash for handshake validation.
    info_hash: [u8; 20],

    /// Number of pieces in the torrent.
    ///
    /// Used to size the bitfield in `PeerState` for new connections.
    num_pieces: usize,

    /// Maximum number of concurrent connections.
    max_connections: usize,
}

impl PeerConnectionManager {
    /// Creates a new PeerConnectionManager.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `peer_id` - Our 20-byte peer ID for handshakes
    /// * `info_hash` - The 20-byte info hash for this torrent
    /// * `num_pieces` - Total number of pieces in the torrent
    /// * `max_connections` - Maximum number of concurrent connections
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::{PeerConnectionManager, DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS};
    ///
    /// let manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
    ///     [0xAA; 20],
    ///     [0xBB; 20],
    ///     100,
    ///     DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS,
    /// );
    /// ```
    pub fn new(
        peer_id: [u8; 20],
        info_hash: [u8; 20],
        num_pieces: usize,
        max_connections: usize,
    ) -> Self {
        PeerConnectionManager {
            connections: HashMap::new(),
            available_peers: VecDeque::new(),
            peer_id,
            info_hash,
            num_pieces,
            max_connections,
        }
    }

    /// Adds peer addresses to the available queue.
    ///
    /// Addresses are added to the back of the queue (FIFO ordering).
    /// Duplicate addresses are filtered out: we skip any address that
    /// is already in the available queue or already connected.
    ///
    /// Returns the number of peers actually added (excluding duplicates).
    /// This allows callers to log useful information like "Added 3/10 peers
    /// from tracker (7 duplicates)".
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `peers` - Vector of socket addresses to add
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// Number of peers actually added to the available queue.
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```
    /// use std::net::SocketAddr;
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::{PeerConnectionManager, DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS};
    ///
    /// let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
    ///     [0xAA; 20],
    ///     [0xBB; 20],
    ///     100,
    ///     DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS,
    /// );
    ///
    /// let peers: Vec<SocketAddr> = vec![
    ///     "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap(),
    ///     "192.168.1.2:6881".parse().unwrap(),
    /// ];
    /// let added = manager.add_peers(peers);
    ///
    /// assert_eq!(added, 2);
    /// assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 2);
    /// ```
    pub fn add_peers(&mut self, peers: Vec<SocketAddr>) -> usize {
        let mut added = 0;
        for addr in peers {
            // Skip if already connected
            if self.connections.contains_key(&addr) {
                continue;
            }
            // Skip if already in available queue.
            // Note: O(n) check on VecDeque. If profiling shows this is slow
            // with large peer lists, consider adding a HashSet for O(1) lookup.
            if self.available_peers.contains(&addr) {
                continue;
            }
            self.available_peers.push_back(addr);
            added += 1;
        }
        added
    }

    /// Returns the number of active connections.
    #[inline]
    pub fn active_count(&self) -> usize {
        self.connections.len()
    }

    /// Returns the number of available peer addresses.
    #[inline]
    pub fn available_count(&self) -> usize {
        self.available_peers.len()
    }

    /// Returns a reference to a connection by address.
    ///
    /// Returns `None` if we're not connected to this address.
    #[inline]
    pub fn get_connection(&self, addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<&PeerConnection> {
        self.connections.get(addr)
    }

    /// Returns a mutable reference to a connection by address.
    ///
    /// Returns `None` if we're not connected to this address.
    #[inline]
    pub fn get_connection_mut(&mut self, addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<&mut PeerConnection> {
        self.connections.get_mut(addr)
    }

    /// Removes and returns a connection by address.
    ///
    /// Returns `None` if we're not connected to this address.
    /// After removal, the slot becomes available for a new connection.
    pub fn remove_connection(&mut self, addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<PeerConnection> {
        self.connections.remove(addr)
    }

    /// Returns true if we need (and can) connect to more peers.
    ///
    /// This returns true when:
    /// 1. We have fewer than `max_connections` active connections
    /// 2. There are available peer addresses to connect to
    ///
    /// The main event loop can use this to decide when to initiate
    /// new connections.
    #[inline]
    pub fn needs_more_connections(&self) -> bool {
        self.active_count() < self.max_connections && self.available_count() > 0
    }

    /// Connects to a specific peer address.
    ///
    /// This is a placeholder implementation for task-0015. It creates
    /// a `PeerConnection` with an initialized `PeerState` but does not
    /// establish an actual TCP connection. The real TCP connection logic
    /// will be implemented in task-0101/task-0112.
    ///
    /// # Arguments
    ///
    /// * `addr` - The socket address to connect to
    ///
    /// # Returns
    ///
    /// * `Ok(())` - Connection was created successfully
    /// * `Err(PeerError::AlreadyConnected)` - Already connected to this address
    ///
    /// # Example
    ///
    /// ```no_run
    /// use std::net::SocketAddr;
    /// use literate_bittorrent::client::{PeerConnectionManager, DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS};
    ///
    /// async fn example() {
    ///     let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
    ///         [0xAA; 20],
    ///         [0xBB; 20],
    ///         100,
    ///         DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS,
    ///     );
    ///
    ///     let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
    ///     manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await.unwrap();
    ///
    ///     assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 1);
    ///     assert!(manager.get_connection(&addr).is_some());
    /// }
    /// ```
    pub async fn connect_to_peer(&mut self, addr: SocketAddr) -> Result<(), PeerError> {
        // Check for duplicate connection
        if self.connections.contains_key(&addr) {
            return Err(PeerError::AlreadyConnected);
        }

        // Create peer state with proper bitfield sizing
        let state = PeerState::new(self.num_pieces as u32);

        // Create the connection (TCP stream will be added in task-0112)
        let conn = PeerConnection { addr, state };

        // Add to active connections
        self.connections.insert(addr, conn);

        Ok(())
    }

    /// Pops the next available peer address from the queue.
    ///
    /// Returns `None` if the available queue is empty.
    /// This is useful for the connection loop to get addresses
    /// to connect to.
    pub fn pop_available(&mut self) -> Option<SocketAddr> {
        self.available_peers.pop_front()
    }

    /// Returns our peer ID.
    #[inline]
    pub fn peer_id(&self) -> &[u8; 20] {
        &self.peer_id
    }

    /// Returns the info hash.
    #[inline]
    pub fn info_hash(&self) -> &[u8; 20] {
        &self.info_hash
    }

    /// Returns the maximum number of connections.
    #[inline]
    pub fn max_connections(&self) -> usize {
        self.max_connections
    }

    /// Returns an iterator over all active connections.
    pub fn connections(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = (&SocketAddr, &PeerConnection)> {
        self.connections.iter()
    }

    /// Returns a mutable iterator over all active connections.
    pub fn connections_mut(&mut self) -> impl Iterator<Item = (&SocketAddr, &mut PeerConnection)> {
        self.connections.iter_mut()
    }
}
@

\subsubsection{Connection Manager Tests}

We test the [[PeerConnectionManager]] to ensure it correctly manages
the connection pool and peer queues.

<<connection manager tests>>=
    // ========================================================================
    // PeerConnectionManager Tests
    // ========================================================================

    #[test]
    fn manager_new_creates_empty_manager() {
        let manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS,
        );

        assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 0);
        assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 0);
        assert_eq!(manager.max_connections(), DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS);
        assert_eq!(manager.peer_id(), &[0xAA; 20]);
        assert_eq!(manager.info_hash(), &[0xBB; 20]);
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_add_peers_adds_to_available() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let peers: Vec<SocketAddr> = vec![
            "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap(),
            "192.168.1.2:6881".parse().unwrap(),
            "192.168.1.3:6881".parse().unwrap(),
        ];
        manager.add_peers(peers);

        assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 3);
        assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 0);
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_add_peers_filters_duplicates() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
        manager.add_peers(vec![addr]);
        manager.add_peers(vec![addr]); // Duplicate

        assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 1);
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_active_count_tracks_connections() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        // Use a runtime for async
        let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()
            .enable_all()
            .build()
            .unwrap();

        rt.block_on(async {
            let addr1: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
            let addr2: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.2:6881".parse().unwrap();

            manager.connect_to_peer(addr1).await.unwrap();
            assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 1);

            manager.connect_to_peer(addr2).await.unwrap();
            assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 2);
        });
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_available_count_tracks_queue() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 0);

        let peers: Vec<SocketAddr> = vec![
            "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap(),
            "192.168.1.2:6881".parse().unwrap(),
        ];
        manager.add_peers(peers);

        assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 2);

        // Pop one
        manager.pop_available();
        assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 1);
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_needs_more_connections_true_when_under_max() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            2, // Max 2 connections
        );

        // No available peers yet
        assert!(!manager.needs_more_connections());

        // Add some peers
        manager.add_peers(vec!["192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap()]);
        assert!(manager.needs_more_connections());
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_needs_more_connections_false_when_at_max() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            1, // Max 1 connection
        );

        let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()
            .enable_all()
            .build()
            .unwrap();

        rt.block_on(async {
            // Add peers and connect to one
            manager.add_peers(vec![
                "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap(),
                "192.168.1.2:6881".parse().unwrap(),
            ]);
            manager.connect_to_peer("192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap()).await.unwrap();

            // At max connections now
            assert!(!manager.needs_more_connections());

            // Note: available_count is still 2 because connect_to_peer doesn't
            // consume from available_peers. But we're at max connections.
            assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 2);
        });
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_get_connection_returns_connection() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()
            .enable_all()
            .build()
            .unwrap();

        rt.block_on(async {
            let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
            manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await.unwrap();

            let conn = manager.get_connection(&addr);
            assert!(conn.is_some());
            assert_eq!(conn.unwrap().addr, addr);

            // Non-existent address
            let other: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.2:6881".parse().unwrap();
            assert!(manager.get_connection(&other).is_none());
        });
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_get_connection_mut_allows_modification() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()
            .enable_all()
            .build()
            .unwrap();

        rt.block_on(async {
            let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
            manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await.unwrap();

            // Modify the peer state
            let conn = manager.get_connection_mut(&addr).unwrap();
            conn.state.peer_choking = false;

            // Verify the change persisted
            let conn = manager.get_connection(&addr).unwrap();
            assert!(!conn.state.peer_choking);
        });
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_remove_connection_removes_and_returns() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()
            .enable_all()
            .build()
            .unwrap();

        rt.block_on(async {
            let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
            manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await.unwrap();
            assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 1);

            let removed = manager.remove_connection(&addr);
            assert!(removed.is_some());
            assert_eq!(removed.unwrap().addr, addr);
            assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 0);

            // Double remove returns None
            assert!(manager.remove_connection(&addr).is_none());
        });
    }

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn connect_to_peer_adds_connection() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
        manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await.unwrap();

        assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 1);
        let conn = manager.get_connection(&addr).unwrap();
        assert_eq!(conn.addr, addr);

        // PeerState should be initialized correctly
        assert!(conn.state.am_choking);
        assert!(!conn.state.am_interested);
        assert!(conn.state.peer_choking);
        assert!(!conn.state.peer_interested);
    }

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn connect_to_peer_rejects_duplicate() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
        manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await.unwrap();

        // Try to connect again
        let result = manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await;
        assert!(matches!(result, Err(PeerError::AlreadyConnected)));

        // Still only one connection
        assert_eq!(manager.active_count(), 1);
    }

    #[tokio::test]
    async fn connect_to_peer_initializes_bitfield_correctly() {
        let num_pieces = 100;
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            num_pieces,
            30,
        );

        let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
        manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await.unwrap();

        let conn = manager.get_connection(&addr).unwrap();
        // Bitfield should be sized for 100 pieces: ceil(100/8) = 13 bytes
        assert_eq!(conn.state.bitfield.len(), 13);
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_pop_available_returns_fifo_order() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let addr1: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
        let addr2: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.2:6881".parse().unwrap();
        let addr3: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.3:6881".parse().unwrap();

        manager.add_peers(vec![addr1, addr2, addr3]);

        assert_eq!(manager.pop_available(), Some(addr1));
        assert_eq!(manager.pop_available(), Some(addr2));
        assert_eq!(manager.pop_available(), Some(addr3));
        assert_eq!(manager.pop_available(), None);
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_add_peers_skips_already_connected() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()
            .enable_all()
            .build()
            .unwrap();

        rt.block_on(async {
            let addr: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
            manager.connect_to_peer(addr).await.unwrap();

            // Try to add the connected address to available
            manager.add_peers(vec![addr]);

            // Should not be in available queue
            assert_eq!(manager.available_count(), 0);
        });
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_connections_iterator() {
        let mut manager = PeerConnectionManager::new(
            [0xAA; 20],
            [0xBB; 20],
            100,
            30,
        );

        let rt = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread()
            .enable_all()
            .build()
            .unwrap();

        rt.block_on(async {
            let addr1: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.1:6881".parse().unwrap();
            let addr2: SocketAddr = "192.168.1.2:6881".parse().unwrap();

            manager.connect_to_peer(addr1).await.unwrap();
            manager.connect_to_peer(addr2).await.unwrap();

            let addrs: Vec<_> = manager.connections().map(|(a, _)| *a).collect();
            assert_eq!(addrs.len(), 2);
            assert!(addrs.contains(&addr1));
            assert!(addrs.contains(&addr2));
        });
    }

    #[test]
    fn manager_default_constants() {
        assert_eq!(DEFAULT_MAX_CONNECTIONS, 30);
        assert_eq!(CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECS, 10);
    }

